93
00:08:53.636 --> 00:08:57.909
reconcile amounts before and after the
join. If you accepted

94
00:08:57.909 --> 00:09:02.182
the result because the query succeeded,
revise your criterion: successful

95
00:09:02.182 --> 00:09:06.455
execution is not data validity. If the key
is unsafe,

96
00:09:06.455 --> 00:09:10.727
use an approved stable identifier or
report that the available

97
00:09:10.727 --> 00:09:15.000
data cannot support the join.

98
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:19.357
Preserve failure evidence before
attempting repair. Save the command or

99
00:09:19.357 --> 00:09:23.714
query, exact error, relevant input,
environment, and smallest reproduction.

100
00:09:23.714 --> 00:09:28.071
Classify the responsible layer:
expectation, code, data, dependency,

101
00:09:28.071 --> 00:09:32.429
permission, network, configuration, or
environment. Return to the last verified

102
00:09:32.429 --> 00:09:36.786
increment and change the narrowest
responsible layer. Do not delete

103
00:09:36.786 --> 00:09:41.143
a test, weaken an assertion, discard
inconvenient rows, increase a

104
00:09:41.143 --> 00:09:45.500
timeout, or regenerate the entire solution
merely to obtain green

105
00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.857
output. Rerun the failed check,
neighboring regression checks, and the

106
00:09:49.857 --> 00:09:54.214
original acceptance checks. If the
environment is uncertain, stop and

107
00:09:54.214 --> 00:09:58.571
compare versions and configuration before
changing code. If recovery requires

108
00:09:58.571 --> 00:10:02.929
destructive action, production access, new
permissions, or cost beyond the

109
00:10:02.929 --> 00:10:07.286
work order, preserve state and escalate.
Use version control or

110
00:10:07.286 --> 00:10:11.643
a verified backup to reverse unsafe work,
but do not

111
00:10:11.643 --> 00:10:16.000
erase logs or artifacts needed to
understand the failure.

112
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.143
Review security, privacy, and downstream
impact before accepting the work.

113
00:10:20.143 --> 00:10:24.286
Inspect authentication, authorization,
input validation, output encoding,

114
00:10:24.286 --> 00:10:28.429
secrets, logs, dependencies, network
calls, file targets, data exposure, error

115
00:10:28.429 --> 00:10:32.571
handling, and failure defaults. Generated
code may compile while bypassing

116
00:10:32.571 --> 00:10:36.714
an account state or logging a credential.
A statistically correct

117
00:10:36.714 --> 00:10:40.857
transformation may still expose personal
data, erase a protected group,

118
00:10:40.857 --> 00:10:45.000
use incompatible units, or support a
conclusion beyond the sample.

119
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.143
Scan the diff and outputs for sensitive
material. Confirm the

120
00:10:49.143 --> 00:10:53.286
reviewer, deployment boundary, backup,
monitoring, and rollback evidence.

121
00:10:53.286 --> 00:10:57.429
Destructive, external, costly, or
permission-expanding actions require the

122
00:10:57.429 --> 00:11:01.571
authorized workflow and explicit approval;
model confidence never substitutes for

123
00:11:01.571 --> 00:11:05.714
authority. Record the work order,
inspected inputs, diff or query,

124
00:11:05.714 --> 00:11:09.857
check outputs, failure-and-recovery
evidence, limitations, reviewer, and

125
00:11:09.857 --> 00:11:14.000
disposition. Commit or publish only the
verified scoped result.

126
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:18.385
The baseline policy depends on processing
order. First require the

127
00:11:18.385 --> 00:11:22.769
exact five-field header. Python's
DictReader exposes the detected header

128
00:11:22.769 --> 00:11:27.154
through reader.fieldnames, so the program
can compare the names and

129
00:11:27.154 --> 00:11:31.538
